---
import DocsLayout from "../../../components/DocsLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const bundle = `<script src="./vendor/mdbase-sdk.min.js"></script>
<script>
  const { connect, Client, AppHost } = window.MdbaseSDK;
  // connect and the other root exports match @mdbase-dev/sdk.
  // Client groups the client classes; AppHost matches @mdbase-dev/sdk/app-host.
</script>`;

const copy = `npm install @mdbase-dev/sdk
cp node_modules/@mdbase-dev/sdk/dist/browser/mdbase-sdk.min.js public/vendor/`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Browser bundle"
  description="Use the SDK from a plain script tag, without a bundler."
  active="portable-apps"
  headings={[
    { href: "#bundle", label: "Classic-script bundle" },
    { href: "#status", label: "Downloaded HTML files" }
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Most apps import <code>@mdbase-dev/sdk</code> through a bundler. If yours
    doesn’t use one, the package also ships a classic-script build.
  </p>

  <h2 id="bundle">Classic-script bundle</h2>
  <p>
    The package includes <code>dist/browser/mdbase-sdk.js</code> and a minified
    {" "}<code>mdbase-sdk.min.js</code>. Copy one into your app and serve it from
    your own origin. mdbase does not publish it on a CDN.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={copy} language="sh" label="Terminal" />
  <CodeBlock code={bundle} language="html" label="index.html" />
  <p>
    Loading the bundle does nothing on its own. It doesn’t open a session,
    touch storage or grant access. Module code can import the same surface from
    {" "}<code>@mdbase-dev/sdk/browser</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="status">Downloaded HTML files</h2>
  <p>
    The manifest format has a <code>"portable"</code> distribution for a
    single HTML file that runs without a web origin. The launch SDK has no
    sign-in flow for portable files, so this guide doesn’t cover one. Build a
    web app with an HTTPS origin instead.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
